From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 18 Aug 2026 05:10:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 31/31] fbgfx: ask the kernel for the depth instead of assuming
 32bpp

This driver already takes the framebuffer's address, width, height and pitch
from KrnGetSystemAttr(). The depth was the one property it hard-coded, which
quietly made it a driver for one bootloader's choice rather than for a linear
framebuffer -- and KATTR_FrameBufferDepth has existed in aros/kernel.h all
along, unread.

Reading it lets the same driver serve m68k-emu68, where Emu68 hands over a
16-bit 5:6:5 surface instead of the firmware's 32-bit R,G,B,x. A kernel with
nothing to say answers -1, and 32bpp is what this file assumed before, so that
remains the default and the aarch64 target's behaviour is unchanged.

Two things follow from supporting 16bpp, and only one of them is arithmetic.

The masks and shifts differ. AROS's MAP_COLCOMP convention puts the component
in the top byte -- (mask << shift) == 0xFF000000 -- so the shift is not the
mask's own bit position, which the existing comment already warned about.

The other is byte order. A 5:6:5 pixel is a halfword, so unlike the 32bpp case
(whose masks describe byte order and therefore need nothing) it is sensitive to
the CPU's endianness, and the VideoCore stores it little-endian whatever the
CPU is. On a big-endian target every pixel needs its two bytes swapped, which
is what aHidd_PixFmt_SwapPixelBytes is for; without it int_map_truecolor()
skips the swap it performs for every other big-endian target's little-endian
format and every colour arrives reversed. HWData gains a flag for it, set by
initFBGfxHW() because that is the only part of this driver that knows what the
hardware produced, and forwarded by the display class without further opinion.

fbDoRefreshArea() needed nothing: it copies bytesperpix-sized pixels row by
row and was already format-agnostic.

Not yet run on hardware -- this compiles and links for m68k, and the arithmetic
above is the part most likely to be wrong in a way only a screen will show.
It also stops being silent. Every message in this driver was D() and compiled
out, so a boot log said nothing about the display at all -- not which driver
initialised, not the mode, not the address. The newest component in the system
was the only one that never announced itself, and "which graphics driver is
this boot running?" was answerable only by remembering what had been compiled.
One unconditional line at init fixes that, and it reports the depth this patch
teaches the driver to ask for.

---
--- a/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_support.h
+++ b/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_support.h
@@ -24,6 +24,12 @@
     ULONG	 greenshift;
     ULONG	 blueshift;
     ULONG	 bytesperline;
+    /* TRUE when the surface's pixels are stored in the opposite byte order to
+       the CPU's, which a 16-bit 5:6:5 halfword on a big-endian target is. The
+       display class hands this to aHidd_PixFmt_SwapPixelBytes; deciding it
+       belongs in initFBGfxHW(), which is the only part of this driver that
+       knows what the hardware actually produced. */
+    BOOL	 swappixelbytes;
     BOOL	 owned;
     UBYTE	 palettewidth;
     UBYTE	 DAC[768];
--- a/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_support.c
+++ b/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_support.c
@@ -52,25 +52,72 @@
     data->width        = KrnGetSystemAttr(KATTR_FrameBufferWidth);
     data->height       = KrnGetSystemAttr(KATTR_FrameBufferHeight);
     data->bytesperline = KrnGetSystemAttr(KATTR_FrameBufferPitch);
-    data->depth        = 32;
-    data->bitsperpixel = 32;
-    data->bytesperpixel = 4;
 
     /*
-     * The VideoCore surface displays byte order R,G,B,x (see fb.c put()), so
-     * the pixel masks are red@0-7, green@8-15, blue@16-23. AROS's MAP_COLCOMP
-     * shift convention is (mask << shift) == 0xFF000000, i.e. shift = 24 minus
-     * the mask's bit position - NOT the bit position itself.
+     * Ask, rather than assume. This driver already takes width, height and
+     * pitch from the kernel; the depth was the one thing it hard-coded, which
+     * made it a driver for one bootloader's choice instead of for a linear
+     * framebuffer. A kernel with nothing to say answers -1, and 32bpp is what
+     * this file assumed before, so that stays the default.
      */
-    data->redmask   = 0x000000FF; data->redshift   = 24;
-    data->greenmask = 0x0000FF00; data->greenshift = 16;
-    data->bluemask  = 0x00FF0000; data->blueshift  = 8;
-    data->palettewidth = 8;
+    {
+        IPTR d = KrnGetSystemAttr(KATTR_FrameBufferDepth);
+
+        if (d != 16 && d != 32)
+            d = 32;
+        data->depth = data->bitsperpixel = d;
+        data->bytesperpixel = d >> 3;
+    }
+
+    /*
+     * AROS's MAP_COLCOMP shift convention is (mask << shift) == 0xFF000000 --
+     * the component ends up in the top byte -- so the shift is *not* the
+     * mask's own bit position.
+     */
+    if (data->bitsperpixel == 16)
+    {
+        /*
+         * 5:6:5 in a halfword. Emu68 hands this over on the Raspberry Pi and
+         * calls it RGB16_LE; the VideoCore stores it little-endian whatever
+         * the CPU is, so on a big-endian target every pixel needs its two
+         * bytes swapped on the way through. Getting this wrong is not subtle
+         * and not silent -- every colour comes out wrong.
+         */
+        data->redmask   = 0x0000F800; data->redshift   = 16;
+        data->greenmask = 0x000007E0; data->greenshift = 21;
+        data->bluemask  = 0x0000001F; data->blueshift  = 27;
+        data->palettewidth = 8;
+        data->swappixelbytes = AROS_BIG_ENDIAN ? TRUE : FALSE;
+    }
+    else
+    {
+        /*
+         * The VideoCore surface displays byte order R,G,B,x (see fb.c put()),
+         * so the pixel masks are red@0-7, green@8-15, blue@16-23. Byte order
+         * rather than word layout, so there is nothing to swap.
+         */
+        data->redmask   = 0x000000FF; data->redshift   = 24;
+        data->greenmask = 0x0000FF00; data->greenshift = 16;
+        data->bluemask  = 0x00FF0000; data->blueshift  = 8;
+        data->palettewidth = 8;
+        data->swappixelbytes = FALSE;
+    }
     data->fbsize = data->height * data->bytesperline;
 
-    D(bug("[FBGfx] HwInit: %ux%ux%u linear FB @ 0x%p, pitch %u\n",
-          data->width, data->height, data->depth,
-          data->framebuffer, data->bytesperline));
+    /*
+     * Unconditional, unlike everything else here.
+     *
+     * Every other message in this driver is D() and compiled out, so a boot
+     * log had nothing at all to say about the display -- not which driver
+     * initialised, not at what mode, not where. That is one line's worth of
+     * silence hiding the newest component in the system, and it made a
+     * question as basic as "which graphics driver is this boot running?"
+     * answerable only by remembering what was compiled.
+     */
+    bug("[FBGfx] %ux%u %ubpp linear FB @ 0x%p, pitch %u%s\n",
+        data->width, data->height, data->bitsperpixel,
+        data->framebuffer, data->bytesperline,
+        data->swappixelbytes ? ", byte-swapped" : "");
 
     ClearBuffer(data);
     return TRUE;
--- a/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_displayclass.c
+++ b/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_displayclass.c
@@ -52,6 +52,14 @@
         {aHidd_PixFmt_CLUTShift,    0}, /* 13 */
         {aHidd_PixFmt_CLUTMask,     0}, /* 14 */
         {aHidd_PixFmt_BitMapType,   vHidd_BitMapType_Chunky}, /* 15 */
+        /*
+         * Set for a surface whose pixels are stored in the opposite byte order
+         * to the CPU's -- a 5:6:5 halfword on a big-endian target. Without it
+         * int_map_truecolor() skips the swap it performs for every other
+         * big-endian target's little-endian formats and every colour arrives
+         * with its bytes reversed. initFBGfxHW() decides; this only forwards.
+         */
+        {aHidd_PixFmt_SwapPixelBytes, 0}, /* 16 */
         {TAG_DONE, 0UL }
     };
     struct TagItem sync_mode[] =
@@ -95,6 +103,7 @@
     pftags[10].ti_Data = XSD(cl)->data.bytesperpixel;
     pftags[11].ti_Data = (XSD(cl)->data.bitsperpixel > 24) ? 24 : XSD(cl)->data.bitsperpixel;
     pftags[14].ti_Data = (1 << XSD(cl)->data.depth) - 1;
+    pftags[16].ti_Data = XSD(cl)->data.swappixelbytes;
 
     sync_mode[0].ti_Data = 60 * XSD(cl)->data.width * XSD(cl)->data.height;
     sync_mode[1].ti_Data = XSD(cl)->data.width;
